# Docs mission profile — sourced by tools/launchd/mission-control.sh when launchd
# sets MISSION_PROFILE=docs. Created 2026-08-28 (Mark, attended).
#
# THIS IS A VERSIONED COPY, NOT THE LIVE FILE. The driver sources
# ~/.config/ailang/mission-docs.env. Editing this file changes nothing on the rig —
# copy it across to deploy. (mission-env/README.md.)
#
# Convention (LOAD-BEARING, not style): every entry uses ${VAR:-value} so a
# command-line pin and pin-root.sh's exported MISSION_WORKDIR both still win. A bare
# assignment silently overrides its own caller — that is the bug that made the World
# loop's "driver pin:" line name the stale clone for weeks. The four identity vars
# below are the documented exception (the bootstrap guide's Step 3 shape).

MISSION_NAME=docs
MISSION_REPO=sunholo-data/ailang
MISSION_DOC=design_docs/docs-mission.md
# Its OWN clone. The website lives in this repo, which the V1 loop is also mutating
# every 90 minutes; two loops in one working tree is the hazard the concurrent-agent
# rule exists for. Separate clone = the motoko precedent.
MISSION_WORKDIR="${MISSION_WORKDIR:-/Users/voightkampff/dev/sunholo-data/ailang-docs}"

# --- PATH (the World iter-23 lesson, paid forward) ----------------------------
# The shared driver only PREPENDS $HOME/go/bin:$HOME/.local/bin to $PATH. A plist
# that carries no PATH leaves it homebrew-less, so `codex`, `gh`, `go` and `node`
# all resolve rc=127, every model pre-flight false-negatives, and every role
# silently degrades to opus — the exact opposite of this mission's purpose. The
# plist next door DOES set PATH; this line is the belt to that braces, because the
# failure is silent and reads like a quota outage.
PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:$PATH

# =============================================================================
# ROUTING — subscription-first ladder (Mark, attended 2026-08-28)
# =============================================================================
# Mark's shape: "sonnet, openai cheap tiers, with fallbacks to ollama equivalent,
# and then fallback to glm 5.3 flash".
#
# The ordering principle is COST TYPE, not model quality: spend the subscriptions
# already paid for, then the flat-rate route, and only reach metered dollars at the
# very last rung. Every step down is cheaper than the one above it in real money.
#
#   rung 1  sonnet / codex          subscription  (already paid for)
#   rung 2  *:cloud                 flat-rate     (Ollama Cloud pro)
#   rung 3  openrouter twin         metered       (last resort, cheapest available)
#
# ANSWERING "or what openrouter equivalents?": Ollama Cloud serves glm-5.3-flash
# ITSELF (probed 2026-08-28, rc=0), and its OpenRouter twin is `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash`
# — the SAME WEIGHTS on a metered route. So rungs 2 and 3 are one model on two
# routes: exhausting the flat-rate quota costs money, never capability. That is the
# fleet's established pattern, not a new idea.
#
# Live prices read from the OpenRouter models API 2026-08-28 ($/M in / out):
#   z-ai/glm-5.3-flash                 0.075 / 0.250   ctx 1,310,720
#   deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731    0.060 / 0.120   ctx 1,310,720
#   minimax/minimax-m3                 0.300 / 1.200
#   openai/gpt-5.6-luna                0.200 / 1.200
#   moonshotai/kimi-k3                 3.000 /15.000   <- NOT cheap; see planner note
#
# deepseek-v4-flash-0731 is marginally cheaper than glm-5.3-flash, but glm-5.3-flash
# is the newer generation at a trivial delta on this mission's volume, and Mark named
# it. Noted so the choice reads as a decision rather than an oversight.
#
# BOTH NEW RUNGS PROBED LIVE before being written here (rc=0 each):
#   codex exec --model gpt-5.6-luna         -> "ok", 7,930 tokens, subscription lane
#   ollama run glm-5.3-flash:cloud          -> "ok"

# Controller — the driver's controller ladder only speaks Anthropic model IDs
# (MISSION_MODEL_PREFS) and ONE codex fallback; it cannot express the ollama/openrouter
# rungs. So the controller gets rungs 1a and 1b of the ladder and stops there.
MISSION_MODEL_PREFS="${MISSION_MODEL_PREFS:-claude-sonnet-5}"
MISSION_CONTROLLER_FALLBACK="${MISSION_CONTROLLER_FALLBACK:-codex:gpt-5.6-luna}"

# Designer — seed only. The designer is a skill-side ROTATION, not a chain: there is no
# MISSION_DESIGNER_FALLBACK in the driver. Seeded at sonnet (rung 1) rather than the
# fable default; the rotation's other entry is a flat-rate cloud lane, which is rung 2.
# Most docs items need no design doc at all — see the charter's Guardrails.
MISSION_DESIGNER_MODEL="${MISSION_DESIGNER_MODEL:-claude:claude-sonnet-5}"

# Planner — starts at rung 1b, NOT sonnet, and that is FORCED rather than chosen:
# derive-planner-lane.sh Step 0 accepts only `codex:*` or `pi:*`, so a bare `sonnet`
# planner pin emits "opus fail-closed:env-pin" and silently runs OPUS — the most
# expensive model in the fleet, on the mission built to avoid it.
#
# The chain also drops kimi-k3, which is the FLEET default here. On the flat-rate route
# kimi is free, but its OpenRouter twin is $3/$15 per M — 40x glm-5.3-flash and more
# expensive than gpt-5.6-sol. As the last rung of a cost ladder that is backwards, so
# this mission ends on glm-5.3-flash instead.
MISSION_PLANNER_MODEL="${MISSION_PLANNER_MODEL:-codex:gpt-5.6-luna}"
MISSION_PLANNER_FALLBACK="${MISSION_PLANNER_FALLBACK:-pi:ollama/glm-5.3-flash:cloud,pi:openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash}"

# PLANNER ALLOWLIST — WITHOUT THIS LINE THE WHOLE ROUTING BLOCK ABOVE IS A NO-OP.
#
# derive-planner-lane.sh fails CLOSED to opus unless every path a design doc declares
# clears an allowlist that was, until 2026-08-28, infra-only (tools/launchd/*, two
# skill paths). Every docs design doc declares docs/... — so the pinned cheap planner
# would read as configured in the driver log while OPUS ran, every single iteration.
# Measured that day with a discriminating control: a docs/ doc emitted "opus
# fail-closed:path-not-in-codex-allowlist" while a byte-identical tools/launchd/ doc
# reached the pi lane.
#
# The gate is now per-mission data (default unchanged = infra-only). Widened HERE, and
# only here, to this mission's actual subject matter. It is still an ALLOWLIST:
# internal/**, cmd/**, and `docs/../internal/...` traversal are all still denied — all
# four properties are asserted in tools/launchd/test_mission_routing.sh.
#
# WIDENED tools/launchd/* -> tools/* (Mark, attended 2026-08-28: "sure it can write
# tools"). The iteration-0 design quorum blocked the charter on this, and the
# objection was correct: clause 7 makes "build the inbox routing" a MANDATORY
# deliverable, while the allowlist permitted only `tools/launchd/*` — so a router
# living anywhere else under tools/ was mechanically unbuildable. Verified both
# directions before asking: `tools/messaging/docs_inbox_router.sh` failed closed
# under the old list and reaches the pinned lane under the new one.
#
# The delta is SMALLER than it looks: `tools/launchd/*` already covered the shared
# mission driver and every sibling's env file, so this grants non-launchd subdirs
# of tools/, not new reach into the fleet's control plane. The cross-mission
# contract still applies as POLICY where the allowlist is silent — report defects
# in shared machinery upstream, never edit a sibling mission's files.
#
# Still an allowlist: internal/**, cmd/**, and traversal out of a permitted prefix
# remain denied, all asserted in tools/launchd/test_mission_routing.sh.
MISSION_PLANNER_ALLOWLIST="${MISSION_PLANNER_ALLOWLIST:-tools/*|.claude/skills/mission-control/SKILL.md|.claude/skills/design-doc-creator/*|docs/*|examples/*|README.md|CHANGELOG.md}"

# Executor — the high-VOLUME role, so the ladder pays most here. Same shape as the
# planner; the driver dedupes the shared probe, so the two roles cost one probe.
MISSION_EXECUTOR_MODEL="${MISSION_EXECUTOR_MODEL:-codex:gpt-5.6-luna}"
MISSION_EXECUTOR_FALLBACK="${MISSION_EXECUTOR_FALLBACK:-pi:ollama/glm-5.3-flash:cloud,pi:openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash}"

# Evaluator — sonnet at rung 1, and this is where the ladder is deliberately NOT
# followed downward as far as it could go.
#
# TWO reasons, and the second is structural rather than taste. (1) On a mission whose
# generator is cheap on purpose, the judge is the wrong economy: a cheap executor
# checked by a cheap judge means nobody catches anything and the loop reports success
# it did not earn. (2) GENERATOR != JUDGE. The executor chain is
# OpenAI -> Z-AI -> Z-AI; if the evaluator walked the SAME ladder it would share a
# vendor with the executor at every rung, which is exactly the shared-blind-spot the
# rule exists to prevent. So the evaluator walks a vendor-DISJOINT ladder —
# Anthropic -> MiniMax -> MiniMax — and the two chains never collide at any rung.
MISSION_EVALUATOR_MODEL="${MISSION_EVALUATOR_MODEL:-sonnet}"
MISSION_EVALUATOR_FALLBACK="${MISSION_EVALUATOR_FALLBACK:-pi:ollama/minimax-m3:cloud,pi:openrouter/minimax/minimax-m3}"

# Metered ceiling — 1/5th of the fleet default ($5). Rungs 1 and 2 are subscription and
# flat-rate, so a fire that spends real dollars has already fallen to rung 3; a low
# ceiling turns that into a loud stop rather than a silent bill. Caps DOLLARS, not tokens.
MISSION_METERED_BUDGET_USD="${MISSION_METERED_BUDGET_USD:-1}"
